<h2>What "Sea the Future" was</h2>
<p>"Sea the Future" was a decision-support tool built by the California State Coastal Conservancy and NOAA's Office for Coastal Management to help planners compare 12 sea-level-rise and coastal-flooding visualization tools covering California's coastline. It shut down when its funding ran out, and the live site now 404s. This project reconstructs its core comparison function from the tool's original public spec, published by the Georgetown Climate Center's Adaptation Clearinghouse: <a href="https://www.adaptationclearinghouse.org/resources/california-e-sea-the-future-e-tool.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">read the original spec →</a></p>

<h2>Why this project is two tools, not one</h2>
<p>The map and the tool comparison answer genuinely different questions. The map lets you see what a specific piece of California coastline actually looks like under real flood-hazard data — but only for the tools whose data is legally open to reuse as a live overlay. The comparison grid helps you figure out which of the 12 tools is the right one for your question in the first place, including the ones that will never appear as a map layer here because their own terms of use prohibit it. Neither page tries to be the other; see <code>BRIEF.md</code> in the project's GitHub repository for the full scoping decision and the licensing behind it.</p>

<h2>Not affiliated</h2>
<p>This project is an independent, unofficial recreation. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or produced by the California State Coastal Conservancy, NOAA, the San Francisco Bay Conservation and Development Commission (BCDC), or any of the 12 tools' original developers. Descriptions of each tool are a good-faith summary compiled from each provider's own public materials, not the original agency content — always confirm details on a tool's own site before relying on it for a real planning decision.</p>

